WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:04.930
I want to talk about Tuca and Bertie, the
animated Netflix show, and how it uses a sex

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:08.850
scene to do everything that Fifty Shades wishes
it could have done, but first I’m going

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:14.310
to just shove in a super short lukewarm defence
of gratuitous sex scenes because I don’t

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:18.920
want to give the impression that this is,
like, the only correct way to do things.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:23.760
I have spent a prety substantial amount of
time talking about the fifty shades franchise,

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:28.390
but in all that time I’ve not really spent
much of it discussing most of the sex scenes

00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:31.380
beyond acknowledging that they exist.

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:37.499
A big reason for that is that with few exceptions,
the scenes don’t really do anything.

00:00:37.499 --> 00:00:41.379
This is not a new criticism, of course, plenty
of people have pointed out that the Fifty

00:00:41.379 --> 00:00:45.579
Shades sex scenes largely exist in bubble
space.

00:00:45.579 --> 00:00:48.409
Things stop, sex happens, things move on.

00:00:48.409 --> 00:00:52.910
They are scenes that can be pretty cleanly
excised for different markets without having

00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:55.720
a meaningful impact on the films.

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:57.959
They just don’t have a lot of purpose.

00:00:57.959 --> 00:01:02.539
Now, this is probably something you’ve heard
before, but let’s actually dig into that

00:01:02.539 --> 00:01:06.220
for a second: what gives a sex scene “purpose”?

00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:11.400
I’m going to argue, however briefly, a much
more permissive interpretation of this.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:16.290
Purpose is a narrative construct and exists
in that context.

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:22.390
It is characterization, it is world building,
it is theme, it is plot, but it’s also pacing

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:24.830
and aesthetic fulfillment.

00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:29.730
Looking good and breaking up the action or
drama for a couple minutes as a narrative

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:32.790
exhale are a good enough purpose.

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:37.980
Not every scene in every movie needs to be
deep to justify its existence.

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:41.430
Fun is an acceptable aesthetic goal.

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:46.030
Where Fifty Shades ends up really falling
flat is that the sex scenes are not only isolated

00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:51.530
from plot and theme and character, they’re
also repetitive, overly long, not particularly

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:54.490
good looking, and generally unmemorable.

00:01:54.490 --> 00:02:00.990
Basically the nominal value as aesthetic or
pacing are undercut by not having a good aesthetic

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:02.600
and killing the pacing.

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:07.660
Okay, so let’s talk about a sex scene that’s
the complete opposite of all of that.

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:13.319
Tuca and Bertie is a 2019 Netflix original
animated sitcom created by illustrator, writer,

00:02:13.319 --> 00:02:15.209
and cartoonist Lisa Hanawalt.

00:02:15.209 --> 00:02:19.760
The series revolves around the titular characters
Tuca and Bertie as they navigate the complexities

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:20.760
of adulthood.

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:26.209
Tuca is a fun loving, carefree, directionless
catastrophe living off her rich aunt’s goodwill

00:02:26.209 --> 00:02:32.310
while dealing with hidden pain and trauma,
and Bertie is a nervous, career-minded data

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:37.069
analyst and aspiring baker dealing with hidden
pain and trauma.

00:02:37.069 --> 00:02:41.599
The scene I want to focus on comes from the
third episode, The Deli Guy.

00:02:41.599 --> 00:02:45.680
Since her long term boyfriend Speckle moved
in with her, Bertie feels like their sex life

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:51.629
has grown routine and uninteresting, which
she catasrophizes as the seed of a chain reaction

00:02:51.629 --> 00:02:54.670
of resentment that will eventually ruin her
life.

00:02:54.670 --> 00:03:00.239
Parallel to this Bertie has been developing
a sexual interest in Pastry Pete, an authoritative,

00:03:00.239 --> 00:03:03.170
dominating older celebrity pastry chef.

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:06.969
To spice things up she suggests they try something
new.

00:03:06.969 --> 00:03:11.090
“What if you, I don’t know, ordered me
around?”

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:15.739
Speckle suggests a safeword, Bertie brushes
the suggestion off because she wants to look

00:03:15.739 --> 00:03:19.040
cool and strong, and the roleplay begins.

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:23.260
“Maybe something like: bend over you little
tart!”

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:25.129
“Oh woah, okay”

00:03:25.129 --> 00:03:29.609
At first things are going well but they quickly
take a turn for the worse after Speckle calls

00:03:29.609 --> 00:03:34.950
Bertie a “bad bird” triggering an intensely
negative reaction.

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:39.450
As Bertie breaks down, Speckle uses his warm
potato safe word to end things, and the two

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:43.019
of them agree to just go with the usual for
the night.

00:03:43.019 --> 00:03:44.019
“Usual way!

00:03:44.019 --> 00:03:45.019
Usual way!”

00:03:45.019 --> 00:03:47.529
This scene is brilliant.

00:03:47.529 --> 00:03:52.040
While this specific interaction never comes
up again in the season, the characters never

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:57.999
try again or really talk about what it means,
the themes underlying this scene form a thesis

00:03:57.999 --> 00:04:00.379
statement for the season as a whole.

00:04:00.379 --> 00:04:05.340
Speckle crosses Bertie’s limits, pushes
her boundaries, but doesn’t do so out of

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:08.040
neglect or malice or ego.

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:12.760
He crosses those lines because he doesn’t
know they’re there, and he doesn’t know

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:18.090
where Bertie’s lines are because Bertie
doesn’t know where Bertie’s lines are.

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:20.450
“I didn’t think I’d react this way.

00:04:20.450 --> 00:04:22.020
What the hell?”

00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:26.340
And it’s important to emphasize that these
lines aren’t cleanly drawn.

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:31.520
When Speckle calls Bertie a tart and starts
spanking her, she’s super into it.

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:32.930
“He he he he”

00:04:32.930 --> 00:04:38.390
What Bertie doesn’t understand about herself
is that she likes the idea of submission but

00:04:38.390 --> 00:04:44.170
only in the context of validation and ends
up confronting that about herself the hard

00:04:44.170 --> 00:04:45.510
way: by accident.

00:04:45.510 --> 00:04:48.280
“But I’m a good bird”

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:53.690
Speckle wants to respect Bertie’s boundaries,
but he can’t do that if she doesn’t know

00:04:53.690 --> 00:04:54.760
what they are.

00:04:54.760 --> 00:05:00.470
This, then, is the thesis statement that underpins
the season, that we don't often know what

00:05:00.470 --> 00:05:06.040
our boundaries are until we hit them, whether
it be in matters of sex, family, friends,

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:09.090
co-living, or any other relationship.

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:13.851
Over the course of the season all the characters
confront this realization, that they need

00:05:13.851 --> 00:05:18.590
to get to know themselves better so that they
can actually communicate their wants and needs

00:05:18.590 --> 00:05:20.630
with the people who love them.

00:05:20.630 --> 00:05:24.360
As the season goes on the stakes of these
conflicts escalate.

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:29.370
Tuca needs to confront her history of substance
abuse and her toxic relationship with her

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:30.370
aunt.

00:05:30.370 --> 00:05:34.770
Bertie needs to confront the ways childhood
abuse has echoed through her life.

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:39.900
Speckle comes face to face with the possibility
that no matter how much he loves her, his

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:45.800
emotional needs, where he wants his life to
go, might be incompatible with staying together

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:46.880
with Bertie.

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:52.070
The show does a really fantastic job of getting
into the chaotic mess of being an adult and

00:05:52.070 --> 00:05:57.720
the give and take required to navigate relationships
where both people are willing participants

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:04.300
who have their own complicated wants and needs,
where some boundaries are big and obvious

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:07.650
and others are really detailed and touchy.

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:13.440
Sometimes, between people, those needs line
up, and sometimes they’re mutually exclusive,

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:17.350
and maybe we don’t even entirely understand
them ourselves.

00:06:17.350 --> 00:06:24.850
All of us need to compromise and in turn ask
others to compromise for us.

00:06:24.850 --> 00:06:30.070
Navigating that space between ourselves is
not a clean process, and may in fact be deeply

00:06:30.070 --> 00:06:31.070
painful.

00:06:31.070 --> 00:06:34.680
There are things we’re willing to compromise
on because it will make someone else happy,

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:39.670
and making them happy will make use happier
than keeping the thing we compromised, and

00:06:39.670 --> 00:06:45.110
there are lines that we have to ask others
to never cross, spaces that we have to ask

00:06:45.110 --> 00:06:46.550
others to give up.

00:06:46.550 --> 00:06:52.780
And the worst part is that we may not even
know where those lines and spaces are until

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:56.430
it’s too late and we’ve stumbled into
them by accident.

00:06:56.430 --> 00:07:01.840
All of this Tuca and Bertie manages to touch
on in one ninety second sex scene.

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:06.710
And honestly, I don’t think you could do
the same this efficiently without that aspect,

00:07:06.710 --> 00:07:12.600
without it being a sex scene, and without
the vulnerability that represents.

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:15.020
And that’s what I think really makes this
scene stand out.

00:07:15.020 --> 00:07:20.890
See, while I will defend the gratuitous and
trivial for indulging in aesthetic satiation

00:07:20.890 --> 00:07:26.410
above metaphorical pursuits, this type of
interaction, one that doesn’t just have

00:07:26.410 --> 00:07:32.060
sex as the backdrop for things to happen in
front of, but uses the dynamics of intimacy

00:07:32.060 --> 00:07:37.010
for their intrinsic qualities, it is exemplary
storytelling.

00:07:37.010 --> 00:07:40.310
This is a scene that couldn’t exist in another
context.

00:07:40.310 --> 00:07:45.180
Bertie’s relationship with her own sexuality,
the frame of power dynamics, and the specific

00:07:45.180 --> 00:07:51.970
boundary of being punished in that space,
if you were to de-sex this scene the meaning

00:07:51.970 --> 00:07:53.850
would change entirely.

00:07:53.850 --> 00:07:58.200
This scene takes on heavy implications in
later episodes, when Bertie begins working

00:07:58.200 --> 00:08:04.840
with Pastry Pete who forces her into an uncomfortable
situation with clear sexual undertones, a

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:10.120
moment that exploits Bertie’s own inconfidence
in her boundaries, which spirals into Bertie

00:08:10.120 --> 00:08:13.750
making terrible mistakes that place others
in harm’s way.

00:08:13.750 --> 00:08:20.230
To throw this back to Fifty Shades one last
time, taken as a whole this effectively demonstrates

00:08:20.230 --> 00:08:27.639
the difference between consenting adults exploring
power dynamics and their own wants and needs,

00:08:27.639 --> 00:08:34.310
and abuse, in a way that Fifty Shades never
really managed to do, even when it tried.

00:08:34.310 --> 00:08:37.690
At the end of The Deli Guy Speckle listens
to Bertie.

00:08:37.690 --> 00:08:43.110
She does want him to be in charge, but in
the vein of a Victorian bodice ripper, to

00:08:43.110 --> 00:08:49.330
sweep her off her feet with big, melodramatic
declarations, to be adored.

00:08:49.330 --> 00:08:52.180
But even this comes back later!

00:08:52.180 --> 00:08:56.770
Late in the season Speckle and Bertie toy
with the idea of buying a house and moving

00:08:56.770 --> 00:08:58.570
out of their one bedroom apartment.

00:08:58.570 --> 00:09:05.520
Bertie at one point has a major meltdown and
disappears for several days out of town, during

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:11.390
which Speckle can’t get ahold of her because
she refuses to answer his calls and then loses

00:09:11.390 --> 00:09:13.120
her phone in a lake.

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:17.930
Speckle then buys a fixer upper house when
a sudden opportunity presents itself.

00:09:17.930 --> 00:09:22.330
While he thought that she would be onboard
with this spontaneous move, the stress of

00:09:22.330 --> 00:09:28.390
her vanishing and going incommunicado turns
it into an argument.

00:09:28.390 --> 00:09:30.230
“I can’t just be your rock all the time.

00:09:30.230 --> 00:09:32.910
Sometimes I need you to be my rock.”

00:09:32.910 --> 00:09:35.890
“And I bought a house”
“Wait, what?”

00:09:35.890 --> 00:09:41.290
Speckle needs to confront both Bertie and
himself and admit that while he hoped it would

00:09:41.290 --> 00:09:45.240
make her happy, he wants to move on with his
life.

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:51.260
He hopes that she’ll come with him, but
if she can’t, if their needs have diverged,

00:09:51.260 --> 00:09:56.770
then it’ll be sad, but this is what he needs
to move forward.

00:09:56.770 --> 00:10:03.670
Managing relationships, navigating compromises,
it’s a lot of work, and it is not easy but,

00:10:03.670 --> 00:10:06.640
as Tuca and Bertie argues, it’s worth it.

00:10:06.640 --> 00:10:12.700
We need that human contact, we need those
support networks, flawed and human as they

00:10:12.700 --> 00:10:15.880
may be, because we can’t make it alone.

